# ComposeUX Embedded Display [SPEC:CIO-EMBEDDED-DISPLAY]

> An ESP32 with a TFT display and touch controller becomes a ComposeUX rendering surface and a ComposeIO bus participant. The render engine draws widgets directly to the framebuffer — no LVGL, no platform toolkit. The single board emulates the ComposeIO dual-bus model in software: internal bus for module-to-module communication, external bus via WiFi/MQTT for ecosystem participation. First target: CYD (ESP32-2432S024, ILI9341 240x320, XPT2046 touch).

---

## 1. Design Principles

### [ED-DP-1] Render Engine, Not Toolkit

The display firmware uses the ComposeUX render engine's drawing contract — the same `Canvas` interface implemented by Skia on desktop and Canvas 2D in the browser. No LVGL, no TFT_eSPI widget layer. The ESP32 backend translates drawing primitives to SPI framebuffer writes. Widget code is identical across platforms [DP-CIO-6].

### [ED-DP-2] Two Phases, One Device

Phase 1: standalone render target. The CYD runs the render engine, displays widgets, handles touch. Useful immediately for visual validation — same widgets that render on desktop render on the CYD. No bus participation yet.

Phase 2: fully spec-compliant ComposeIO display device. Adds WiFi/MQTT, bus_storage config pull, document_store asset fetch, namespace binding, and the full boot sequence from [CXC-R-BOOT-SEQUENCE]. The device is fungible — its behavior is determined by the `.compose.yaml` it receives.

### [ED-DP-3] Software Dual-Bus on Single Hardware

A CYD is one board — no physical motherboard/daughterboard separation. The ComposeIO dual-bus model is emulated in software:
- Internal bus: in-process pub/sub (function calls, zero-copy). Display module and touch module communicate here.
- External bus: WiFi/MQTT. Namespace export/import, crossover, `_app/` boundary — all implemented in firmware following the same rules as a Python ComposeApp.
- The board runs both "mainboard" (bus management, crossover, transport) and "daughterboard" (display rendering, touch input) roles in a single firmware image.

### [ED-DP-4] Same Config, Different Hardware

The `.compose.yaml` that drives a CYD is the same format that drives a Python ComposeApp dashboard or a browser widget composition. Only the `device` section differs (resolution, touch type, SPI pins). Widget definitions, namespace bindings, and layout are portable [DP-CIO-1], [DP-CIO-7].

---

## 2. Hardware Profile: CYD (ESP32-2432S024)

### [ED-HW-CYD] CYD Board Specification

| Property | Value |
|----------|-------|
| MCU | ESP32-D0WD-V3 (rev 3.1), dual-core 240MHz, 520KB SRAM |
| Display | ILI9341, 240x320, 16-bit RGB565, SPI (HSPI) |
| Touch | XPT2046 resistive, shared HSPI bus |
| WiFi | 802.11 b/g/n (ESP32 built-in) |
| Storage | SD card slot (VSPI bus, separate from display) |
| LED | RGB (GPIOs 4, 16, 17, active-low) |
| LDR | Photoresistor on GPIO 34 (ADC) |
| Speaker | GPIO 26 (DAC2) |
| Backlight | GPIO 27 (PWM via LEDC, 5kHz) |
| Flash | 4MB QIO, 80MHz |
| USB | CH340C (serial + programming) |
| Exposed GPIOs | P3 connector: GPIO 21, 22, 35 |

### [ED-HW-CYD-PINS] Pin Assignment Table

| Bus | Function | GPIO | Notes |
|-----|----------|------|-------|
| HSPI | Display MOSI | 13 | Shared with touch |
| HSPI | Display MISO | 12 | Shared with touch |
| HSPI | Display SCLK | 14 | Shared with touch |
| HSPI | Display CS | 15 | |
| HSPI | Display DC | 2 | Data/Command select |
| HSPI | Touch CS | 33 | |
| — | Touch IRQ | 36 | Input-only |
| VSPI | SD MOSI | 23 | Separate bus |
| VSPI | SD MISO | 19 | |
| VSPI | SD SCLK | 18 | |
| VSPI | SD CS | 5 | Must stay HIGH during HSPI ops |

### [ED-HW-CYD-QUIRKS] Hardware Quirks

- **MADCTL override required**: Panel scan direction differs from ILI9341 defaults. Custom register 0x36 values: portrait=0x20, landscape=0xE0, portrait-inv=0x60, landscape-inv=0xA0. Standard TFT_eSPI rotation produces garbled output.
- **Color order**: RGB (ILI9341 default is BGR). Must set TFT_RGB_ORDER=1.
- **WiFi timer conflict**: `analogWrite()` and `tone()` conflict with WiFi timers. All PWM must use LEDC API.
- **SD CS isolation**: GPIO 5 must be held HIGH when not accessing SD to prevent HSPI bus interference.
- **Touch calibration**: Hardcoded values `{275, 3620, 264, 3532, 1}` — may need per-unit recalibration.

---

## 3. Phase 1: Standalone Render Target

### [ED-PHASE1] ESP32 Canvas Backend

The ESP32 backend implements the `Canvas` abstract contract from [CXC-R-DRAW-CONTRACT]. It renders to an in-memory framebuffer and flushes to the ILI9341 via SPI DMA.

#### Rendering Strategy

```
Widget.draw(canvas, rect)
  → Canvas drawing commands (fill_rect, fill_arc, draw_text, ...)
  → ESP32 backend writes to RGB565 framebuffer in SRAM
  → present() triggers SPI DMA transfer of dirty regions to ILI9341
```

#### Dirty-Region Tracking

The Canvas backend tracks which scanlines have been modified since the last `present()` call. Every drawing primitive that writes pixels updates a `dirty_min_y` / `dirty_max_y` range (cheapest possible tracking — two integers, zero per-pixel overhead). On `present()`, only the scanline band from `dirty_min_y` to `dirty_max_y` is transferred via SPI DMA. After transfer, the dirty range resets to empty.

This is coarse-grained (full-width scanline bands, not per-pixel rectangles) but sufficient for a 240-pixel-wide display where SPI transfer of a full scanline (480 bytes) is negligible. Finer-grained tracking (per-column dirty rectangles) adds complexity with minimal bandwidth savings at this resolution.

```
// Dirty tracking state in ESP32Canvas
uint16_t _dirty_min_y = UINT16_MAX;  // no dirty region
uint16_t _dirty_max_y = 0;

// Every pixel write calls:
inline void mark_dirty(uint16_t y) {
    if (y < _dirty_min_y) _dirty_min_y = y;
    if (y > _dirty_max_y) _dirty_max_y = y;
}

// present() transfers only dirty band, then resets
void present() {
    if (_dirty_min_y > _dirty_max_y) return;  // nothing dirty
    spi_dma_transfer(framebuffer, _dirty_min_y, _dirty_max_y);
    _dirty_min_y = UINT16_MAX;
    _dirty_max_y = 0;
}
```

| Canvas Method | ESP32 Implementation |
|---------------|---------------------|
| `fill_rect` | Direct framebuffer pixel fill (optimized: memset for solid rows) |
| `fill_circle`, `fill_arc` | Bresenham/midpoint algorithms to framebuffer |
| `draw_line` | Bresenham line to framebuffer |
| `draw_path`, `fill_path` | Scanline fill with edge table |
| `draw_text` | Pre-rendered glyph atlas from bundled TTF (see Font Strategy below) |
| `set_linear_gradient`, `set_radial_gradient` | Per-pixel interpolation during fill |
| `clip_rect` | Scissor bounds check on all pixel writes |
| `save`/`restore` | State stack (small — 8 levels sufficient) |
| `draw_image` | Blit from decoded image buffer in PSRAM |
| `present` | SPI DMA transfer of dirty scanlines to ILI9341 |

#### Memory Budget (240x320 RGB565)

| Resource | Size | Location |
|----------|------|----------|
| Framebuffer | 150KB (240x320x2) | PSRAM (if available) or split into scanline bands |
| Glyph atlas | ~20KB per font size | Flash (compiled in) or PSRAM (fetched) |
| Widget tree | ~2-5KB | SRAM |
| Image cache | Variable | PSRAM |

#### Font Strategy

Pixel-equivalent rendering across platforms requires the same font binary everywhere. The approach:

1. **Bundle a TTF** — Inter or Noto Sans (OFL licensed). The same `.ttf` file is used on desktop (Skia loads it directly), browser (CSS `@font-face`), and ESP32 (pre-rendered to glyph atlas at build time).
2. **Build-time glyph atlas generation** — A build script uses FreeType with deterministic settings (no autohinting, grayscale antialiasing, specific DPI) to render the bundled TTF into a C header containing a bitmap glyph atlas for each required font size. The atlas includes: glyph bitmaps (8-bit alpha), glyph metrics (advance width, bearing), and a character→glyph index lookup.
3. **Runtime text rendering** — `draw_text()` on ESP32 looks up glyphs in the compiled atlas and blits alpha-blended pixels to the framebuffer. No FreeType on device, no runtime font parsing.

This disqualifies Adafruit GFX `fontconvert` — it produces 1-bit glyphs with no antialiasing and its own metrics format, incompatible with the pixel-equivalence goal.

```
fonts/
├── inter_regular.ttf            # Bundled TTF (source of truth)
├── generate_atlas.py            # FreeType script: TTF → C header
└── generated/
    ├── inter_regular_14.h       # Glyph atlas for 14px
    └── inter_regular_16.h       # Glyph atlas for 16px
```

ESP32-D0WD-V3 has 520KB SRAM but no PSRAM on the CYD. The framebuffer (150KB) consumes ~29% of SRAM. Options:
1. Full framebuffer in SRAM (150KB) — leaves ~370KB for app + WiFi stack. Tight but workable.
2. Band rendering: render in horizontal bands (e.g., 16 scanlines = 15KB), transfer via DMA, render next band. Uses 10x less memory but slower (no random-access pixel reads for compositing).

Recommendation: start with full framebuffer. Fall back to band rendering only if memory pressure from WiFi stack forces it.

#### Touch Input

XPT2046 touch events map to the engine's pointer event model from [CXC-R-ENGINE-HIT]:

```
XPT2046 read (raw x, y, pressure)
  → Calibration transform (raw → screen coordinates)
  → Pointer event: {type: down|move|up, x, y, pointer_id: 0}
  → Engine hit test → dispatch to widget
```

Touch polling: 50Hz (20ms interval). Debounce: 50ms for tap detection. Pressure threshold for touch-down vs lift-off.

### [ED-PHASE1-BUILD] Build System

PlatformIO with ESP-IDF framework (not Arduino). ESP-IDF gives direct SPI master driver access for DMA framebuffer transfers, explicit DMA-capable memory allocation (`heap_caps_malloc`), FreeRTOS task pinning (render on core 0, WiFi on core 1 in Phase 2), and WiFi memory tuning. Arduino's TFT_eSPI abstraction is unnecessary — the Canvas backend IS the display driver. The CYD's non-standard MADCTL values require direct register writes regardless.

```
cyd/
├── platformio.ini                   # framework = espidf
├── main/
│   ├── main.cpp                    # App entry: init display, create widget tree, event loop
│   ├── ili9341.cpp                 # ILI9341 driver: SPI master init, command sequences, DMA transfer
│   ├── ili9341.h
│   ├── canvas_esp32.cpp            # ESP32 Canvas backend (draws to framebuffer)
│   ├── canvas_esp32.h
│   ├── touch_xpt2046.cpp           # Touch input driver (SPI reads, calibration)
│   └── touch_xpt2046.h
├── include/
│   ├── cyd_pins.h                  # Pin definitions for CYD board
│   ├── canvas.h                    # Canvas abstract interface (C++ virtual class)
│   ├── widget.h                    # Widget base class (C++ virtual class)
│   └── widgets/                    # Phase 1a: LED widget. Phase 1b: gauge, label, rect, circle
├── fonts/
│   ├── inter_regular.ttf           # Bundled TTF (source of truth for all platforms)
│   ├── generate_atlas.py           # FreeType → C header glyph atlas generator
│   └── generated/
│       └── inter_regular_16.h      # Pre-rendered glyph atlas (build-time generated)
└── generated/
    └── widget_tree.h               # Generated from .compose.yaml by composeio push
```

Phase 1a demo: hardcoded LED widget in `main.cpp` (no config parser). Exercises `fill_circle`, gradient fills, `draw_text`, dirty-region tracking, and SPI DMA transfer — validates the full rendering pipeline from Canvas contract to physical pixels. Phase 1b adds gauge, label, rectangle, and circle widgets to reproduce the desktop demo composition. Visual comparison validates pixel fidelity.

---

## 4. Phase 2: ComposeIO Display Device

### [ED-PHASE2] Full Integration

Phase 2 adds everything needed for the [CXC-R-BOOT-SEQUENCE]:

1. **WiFi connection** (existing in BSP)
2. **MQTT transport** (PubSubClient library or lightweight MQTT client)
3. **Software dual-bus**: internal bus (in-process pub/sub) + external bus (MQTT)
4. **bus_storage client**: HTTP GET for `.compose.yaml` config by UUID
5. **document_store client**: HTTP GET for assets (fonts, icons, images)
6. **Config parser**: `.compose.yaml` → widget tree (compiled by `composeio push` to binary for ESP32, or parsed from YAML at runtime if memory permits)
7. **Namespace binding**: widget ↔ bus namespace wiring
8. **Crossover**: exported namespaces forwarded to MQTT
9. **Config polling**: periodic check for config updates

### [ED-PHASE2-DUAL-BUS] Software Dual-Bus Architecture

```
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  CYD Firmware (single ESP32)                         │
│                                                      │
│  ┌──────────────────────────────────────┐            │
│  │  Internal Bus (in-process)           │            │
│  │                                      │            │
│  │  DisplayModule                       │            │
│  │    subscribes: gauge/*, status/*     │            │
│  │    (render engine drives ILI9341)    │            │
│  │                                      │            │
│  │  TouchModule                         │            │
│  │    publishes: _app/touch/event       │            │
│  │    publishes: button_press (if bound)│────export──┐│
│  │                                      │            ││
│  │  AuxModule (LED, LDR, speaker)       │            ││
│  │    publishes: _app/ambient_light     │            ││
│  │    subscribes: led/color             │            ││
│  └──────────────────────────────────────┘            ││
│                                                      ││
│  ┌──────────────────────────────────────┐            ││
│  │  External Bus (WiFi/MQTT)            │◀───────────┘│
│  │                                      │             │
│  │  button_press ──▶ MQTT               │             │
│  │  gauge/* ◀── MQTT (imported)         │             │
│  └──────────────────────────────────────┘             │
└───────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
```

The software bus is a lightweight C++ implementation:
- Namespace registry: static array (capacity ~32 namespaces for CYD)
- Pub/sub: function pointer callbacks (zero-copy for internal)
- Crossover: table mapping internal ↔ external namespace names
- `_app/` enforcement: prefix check before crossover forwarding

### [ED-PHASE2-CONFIG] Config Compilation

For memory-constrained devices, `.compose.yaml` is compiled to a binary format by `composeio push`:

```
composeio push --target esp32 --device cyd-1 widget_dashboard.compose.yaml
  → Parse YAML
  → Validate namespace contracts
  → Compile widget tree to binary (widget type IDs, property values, namespace hash IDs)
  → Pre-render glyph atlases for fonts at target sizes
  → Upload binary config to bus_storage under device UUID
  → Upload font/image assets to document_store
```

The device fetches the compiled binary config at boot — no YAML parsing on the ESP32.

---

## 5. Auxiliary Peripherals

### [ED-AUX] CYD Peripheral Namespace Mapping

The CYD's non-display peripherals map naturally to ComposeIO namespaces:

| Peripheral | Namespace | Direction | State Engine | Use Case |
|------------|-----------|-----------|-------------|----------|
| RGB LED | `led/color` | Subscribe | JSON `{r, g, b}` | Status indicator driven by bus |
| LDR | `_app/ambient_light` | Publish | continuous_0_255 | Backlight auto-adjust, ambient awareness |
| Backlight | `_app/backlight` | Subscribe | continuous_0_255 | Brightness control (manual or LDR-driven) |
| Speaker | `alert/tone` | Subscribe | JSON `{freq, duration}` | Audible notifications |
| SD Card | (not namespaced) | — | — | Local asset cache, offline config fallback |

The LED, LDR, and speaker are managed by an `AuxModule` on the internal bus. The LDR → backlight binding is a local namespace binding — no MQTT needed, no server, it just works [DP-CIO-10], [DP-CIO-11].

---

## 6. Generalization Beyond CYD

### [ED-GENERAL] Device Profile

The CYD is the first target. The spec applies to any ESP32+display+touch combination. Device-specific details live in a **device profile** section of the `.compose.yaml`:

```yaml
device:
  mcu: esp32
  display:
    controller: ili9341
    width: 240
    height: 320
    color_depth: 16          # RGB565
    spi_bus: hspi
    pins: {cs: 15, dc: 2, mosi: 13, miso: 12, sclk: 14}
    madctl: {portrait: 0x20, landscape: 0xE0}
    backlight: {gpio: 27, pwm_freq: 5000}
  touch:
    controller: xpt2046
    pins: {cs: 33, irq: 36}
    calibration: [275, 3620, 264, 3532, 1]
  peripherals:
    led_rgb: {r: 4, g: 16, b: 17, active_low: true}
    ldr: {gpio: 34}
    speaker: {gpio: 26}
    sd: {cs: 5, spi_bus: vspi}
```

Different boards override the device section. Widget definitions stay the same. A 3.5" ILI9488 480x320 board, a 2.8" ST7789 240x240 round display, or a 7" RA8875 800x480 panel — same render engine, same widget code, different device profile.

---

## 7. Phase Plan

| Phase | Scope | Deliverable |
|-------|-------|-------------|
| 1a | ESP32 Canvas backend (framebuffer + SPI DMA) + one widget | Render an LED widget on CYD — proves drawing primitives (fill_circle, gradients, text), dirty tracking, and DMA pipeline end-to-end |
| 1b | Port remaining Phase 1 widgets (gauge, label, rect, circle) | Desktop demo reproduced on CYD display |
| 1c | Touch input wired to engine | Tap a widget on the CYD, see visual response |
| 2a | WiFi + MQTT transport | CYD publishes touch events to MQTT |
| 2b | bus_storage config pull | CYD fetches .compose.yaml by UUID at boot |
| 2c | document_store asset fetch | CYD fetches fonts and icons over HTTP |
| 2d | Software dual-bus + crossover | Full ComposeIO display device boot sequence |

---

## 8. Compliance [ED-COMPLIANCE]

A compliant embedded display implementation MUST:

1. **[ED-C1]** Render gauges identically on CYD and desktop — visual comparison of same widget config on both surfaces
2. **[ED-C2]** Dispatch touch tap to the correct widget — tap gauge on CYD, widget receives on_tap event
3. **[ED-C3]** Connect WiFi/MQTT (Phase 2) — CYD publishes to MQTT broker, message received
4. **[ED-C4]** Pull config from bus_storage (Phase 2) — change config in bus_storage, CYD renders new widget tree
5. **[ED-C5]** Fetch assets from document_store (Phase 2) — CYD fetches and renders icon from document_store URI
6. **[ED-C6]** Forward crossover events (Phase 2) — touch event on exported namespace appears on MQTT
7. **[ED-C7]** Enforce `_app/` boundary (Phase 2) — `_app/touch/event` does not appear on MQTT
8. **[ED-C8]** Support device fungibility (Phase 2) — two CYDs with different configs render different widget compositions
9. **[ED-C9]** Bind LDR→backlight locally — cover LDR, backlight dims, no WiFi needed
10. **[ED-C10]** Sustain 30fps render (Phase 1) — gauge needle animation at 30fps on CYD

